WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.980
The first commercial hard drive stored five megabytes at a cost that would buy a house, yet

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.380
the same aluminum platters today hold terabytes for pocket change, because engineers kept

00:00:10.380 --> 00:00:13.140
shrinking the bits instead of accepting the old limits.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:16.900
This is First Principles Daily, episode 81.

00:00:17.520 --> 00:00:24.700
In 1956, IBM shipped the Ramack, a refrigerator-sized machine whose 50 spinning aluminum discs

00:00:24.700 --> 00:00:28.680
held five megabytes and sold for tens of thousands of dollars.

00:00:29.480 --> 00:00:35.020
Today, a consumer drive the size of a paperback holds a million times more data for under $50.

00:00:35.620 --> 00:00:39.740
The difference did not come from cheaper aluminum or faster motors alone.

00:00:40.360 --> 00:00:46.000
It came from repeatedly asking how many more magnetic bits could be packed onto the same disc surface

00:00:46.520 --> 00:00:48.560
before the laws of physics intervened.

00:00:48.980 --> 00:00:53.860
That question exposed how much of the original price was tied to mechanical tolerances,

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:57.120
rather than to the aluminum or the magnetic coating itself.

00:00:57.920 --> 00:01:02.520
Early disc drives followed the pattern set by the Ramack and its immediate successors.

00:01:02.980 --> 00:01:08.820
Designers treated the platter diameter, the head flying height, and the inductive coil as fixed elements

00:01:08.820 --> 00:01:13.620
and simply added more platters or larger cabinets when more capacity was needed.

00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:20.020
The recording heads used hand-wound copper coils that sat relatively far above the magnetic surface,

00:01:20.620 --> 00:01:24.800
limiting how small each bit could be made without interference from neighboring bits.

00:01:25.520 --> 00:01:31.260
Servo systems were crude or absent, so tracks had to be spaced widely to guarantee the head

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:34.520
state on target despite vibration and thermal expansion.

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:40.400
Manufacturing therefore centered on precision mechanical assembly of many separate components,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:43.780
each tolerance to avoid crashes or signal loss.

00:01:44.420 --> 00:01:49.860
The result was low aerial density measured in kilobits per square inch, high part counts,

00:01:49.860 --> 00:01:54.480
and prices that scaled roughly with capacity rather than falling ahead of it.

00:01:54.940 --> 00:01:59.780
Because every new drive generation copied the same architecture and merely enlarged it,

00:02:00.120 --> 00:02:04.620
the industry treated the high cost per bit as an unavoidable feature of the technology.

00:02:05.160 --> 00:02:10.440
No one inside the conventional workflow started from the raw question of how many magnetic domains

00:02:10.440 --> 00:02:13.800
could physically occupy one square inch of plated aluminum.

00:02:14.480 --> 00:02:19.100
The assumption was that the head-to-media interface had already reached a practical limit,

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:22.880
set by the materials and machining methods available at the time.

00:02:23.500 --> 00:02:28.920
That assumption kept the idiot index high because most of the expense sat in the assembly tolerances

00:02:28.920 --> 00:02:34.500
and the bulky head structures rather than in the few grams of cobalt, nickel, and aluminum

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:40.500
that actually stored the data. Engineers who later drove the cost curve down began with the

00:02:40.500 --> 00:02:46.380
physical requirement for storing a bit. A small region of magnetic material whose magnetization

00:02:46.380 --> 00:02:52.300
direction could be reliably written and read. The magic wand floor for that bit is essentially

00:02:52.300 --> 00:02:57.980
the cost of a few atoms of cobalt, nickel, or iron, plus the aluminum substrate beneath them.

00:02:58.740 --> 00:03:04.160
Even generous estimates placed the raw material value of a modern platter at only a few dollars

00:03:04.160 --> 00:03:09.900
while the finished drive sells for tens of dollars, producing an idiot index in the single digits

00:03:09.900 --> 00:03:16.260
once capacity is taken into account. The early drives carried a far higher index because most of

00:03:16.260 --> 00:03:21.440
their expense lay in the mechanical tolerances and head design rather than the media itself.

00:03:22.180 --> 00:03:27.100
Thin film heads replaced the wound coils with photolithographically patterned conductors,

00:03:27.460 --> 00:03:33.420
deposited directly on a ceramic slider. This change allowed the head to fly at a few micro-inches

00:03:33.420 --> 00:03:39.020
instead of tens of micro-inches, shrinking the bit size because the magnetic field could be confined

00:03:39.020 --> 00:03:46.040
to a smaller area. A natural objection is that closer flying heights risk head crashes. The redesign

00:03:46.040 --> 00:03:52.080
addressed that by making the slider and disc surfaces smoother through improved polishing and lubrication

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:58.460
rather than by accepting the old clearance. Next, magneto-resistant read heads separated the right

00:03:58.460 --> 00:04:04.240
and read functions so the read sensor could be optimized for sensitivity rather than for generating

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:11.120
a strong field. Giant magneto-resistant heads introduced in the late 1990s stacked alternating

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:17.040
magnetic and non-magnetic layers so that resistance changed dramatically with small external fields,

00:04:17.560 --> 00:04:23.160
permitting still smaller bits to produce usable signals. At the same time embedded servo

00:04:23.160 --> 00:04:29.120
patterns written on the disc itself replaced open loop positioning. The head could now follow tracks

00:04:29.120 --> 00:04:35.340
only a few micrometers wide without mechanical drift. Each of these steps attacked a different physical

00:04:35.340 --> 00:04:42.800
limit. Flying height, signal-to-noise ratio and track following accuracy. Because aerial density is

00:04:42.800 --> 00:04:48.540
the product of bits per inch along a track and tracks per inch, simultaneous gains in both

00:04:48.540 --> 00:04:54.960
dimensions multiplied capacity without enlarging the platter or adding discs. The cost per gigabyte

00:04:54.960 --> 00:05:00.460
therefore fell roughly in proportion to the density increase. Moving the finished product closer

00:05:00.460 --> 00:05:06.540
to the material floor even though the aluminum and magnetic alloys themselves changed only modestly.

00:05:07.300 --> 00:05:12.520
Another objection sometimes raised is that signal processing electronics grew more complex.

00:05:13.200 --> 00:05:18.580
Those electronics however scaled with semiconductor cost curves that were already falling faster

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:23.780
than mechanical assembly costs so the net effect still reduced the overall idiot index.

00:05:24.440 --> 00:05:30.260
The cumulative effect took the cost per gigabyte from millions of dollars in the 1950s to a few

00:05:30.260 --> 00:05:36.740
cents today. Modern drives achieve aerial densities above a terabyte per square inch using the same

00:05:36.740 --> 00:05:42.820
basic aluminum platters and similar total part counts showing that most of the original idiot index

00:05:42.820 --> 00:05:48.860
has been eliminated. Remaining constraints include the superparamagnetic limit where thermal energy

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:54.880
can flip ever smaller bits and the mechanical difficulty of maintaining sub nanometer flying heights

00:05:54.880 --> 00:06:00.960
at higher rotational speeds. Heat assisted and microwave assisted recording are now being explored

00:06:00.960 --> 00:06:06.220
precisely because they address those physical ceilings rather than simply copying prior head

00:06:06.220 --> 00:06:11.980
designs. The tradeoff accepted along the way was greater complexity in the media stack and in the

00:06:11.980 --> 00:06:17.820
signal processing electronics yet those additions still proved cheaper than adding more platters or

00:06:17.820 --> 00:06:24.220
larger cabinets. The remaining gap to the absolute material floor is now set more by the need for

00:06:24.220 --> 00:06:30.320
error correction codes and by the cost of the read channel chips than by the aluminum or magnetic layers

00:06:30.320 --> 00:06:36.520
themselves. One principle visible in the hard drive story is that the dominant cost often hides in

00:06:36.520 --> 00:06:42.680
the spacing between bits rather than in the bits themselves. Closing that spacing required new head

00:06:42.680 --> 00:06:48.900
physics not merely tighter machining of the old heads. A second principle is that once the fundamental

00:06:48.900 --> 00:06:55.960
interaction here magnetic field strength versus distance is made the variable incremental part count

00:06:55.960 --> 00:07:02.000
reductions follow naturally instead of being imposed from above. The same moves could next appear in

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.840
any storage technology whose density is still limited by head media spacing or positioning accuracy.

00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:14.500
Which other data storage formats still carry an idiot index set by yesterday's head design rather

00:07:14.500 --> 00:07:20.500
than by the atoms on the medium. That's first principles daily for today. If this changed how you

